    "stdout_excerpt": "COUNTERFACT_JSON={\"expected\": {\"invalid\": [], \"missing\": [], \"refusal_class\": \"TmxProviderError\", \"refusal_code\": \"TMX_BAD_ROW\", \"rejected\": [], \"status\": null}, \"observed\": {\"invalid\": [], \"missing\": [], \"refusal_class\": \"TmxProviderError\", \"refusal_code\": \"TMX_BAD_ROW\", \"rejected\": [], \"status\": null}, \"reason\": \"symbol is the whole natural key of the index-constituent domain and the only required column of TmxIndexConstituent: nulling it leaves that member's name, exchange, market value and weight intact and leaves the second member complete, so the refusal is about one cell. What the key buys this mapper is *presence*, not uniqueness: the same probe shows two members carrying one symbol still serve two rows, so the mapper must refuse a null-keyed member -- a row a consumer cannot address -- and de-duplication stays the storage layer's job, not a guard this witness could claim; untampered control produced 2 row(s) through the same seam\"}\nVERDICT=PASS\n"
